{
  "dom_id": "step-q173763",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14191213/assets/f0662ae0e6957786ab7f3821a932e1f36a1c8ca6e8299709daa5e323601194fa.png)\n\nIn the figure above, $∠BOA$ is a straight angle, and $\\overset{\\to }{OC}$ is the bisector of $∠BOD.$ If $m∠DOA={84}^{∘},$ then $m∠COA=${{field-1}}\n${}^{∘}.$",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14191213/assets/5291d267ce1e895cfe75261a36c9dbc660a32935b6858ec8986379ee8c8bb766.png)\n\nSince the angles $∠BOD$ and $∠DOA$ are supplementary, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠BOD & ={180}^{∘}-m∠DOA \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{84}^{∘} \\\\  & ={96}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe bisector of an angle divides the angle into two congruent angles. Therefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠COD & =\\frac{m∠BOD}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{96}^{∘}}{2} \\\\  & ={48}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by the angle addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠COA & =m∠COD+m∠DOA \\\\  & ={48}^{∘}+{84}^{∘} \\\\  & ={132}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
